@*
    Provisioning and activation, on a page of their own.

    These two used to be panels at the bottom of the console. They are different in kind from the
    rest of it: everything else there edits one row of one table, while these create an
    application, its account and the mapping between them in a single call, and revoke sessions
    and refresh tokens that have already been handed out. They are also the two the console cannot
    demonstrate — the point of both is that another system can call them — which is why this page
    builds the curl for whatever is in the forms as you fill them in.

    Reachable by the operator account only; see AdminController.IsOperator. Self-contained for the
    same reason Manage.cshtml is: a view rendered out of a NuGet package cannot assume the host has
    a layout, a _ViewStart or tag helpers.
*@
@{
    Layout = null;
    var tenant = ViewBag.Tenant as Ark.oAuth.ArkTenant;
    var title = tenant?.display ?? tenant?.name ?? (ViewBag.TenantId as string);
}
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
    <meta name="robots" content="noindex, nofollow" />
    <meta name="referrer" content="strict-origin" />
    <title>Provisioning — @title</title>
    <link href="@ViewBag.ConsoleCss" rel="stylesheet" />
</head>
<body>
@await Html.PartialAsync("_ConsoleHeader")

<main class="ark-main"
      id="ark-admin"
      data-page="provisioning"
      data-app-root="@ViewBag.AppRoot"
      data-tenant-id="@ViewBag.TenantId"
      data-user-password-mode="@ViewBag.UserPasswordMode"
      data-machine-client="@ViewBag.MachineClientId"
      data-machine-has-secret="@((ViewBag.MachineHasSecret == true).ToString().ToLowerInvariant())"
      data-token-endpoint="@ViewBag.TokenEndpoint"
      data-api-root="@ViewBag.ApiRoot">

    @*
        Provisioning. The four steps below are the ones that have to happen in order for a new
        application to work at all, and the fourth — the access mapping — is the one that gets
        forgotten, because its absence shows up as "that username and password combination was
        not recognised" on the sign-in page rather than as anything about a missing mapping.
        This panel is the whole sequence behind one button, over the same API another system
        would call.
    *@
    <section class="ark-panel">
        <div class="ark-panel-head">
            <h2>Provision an application</h2>
            <span class="ark-hint">registers the client, creates or reuses the user, and maps the two together — in one call</span>
        </div>
        <div class="ark-panel-body">
            <div class="ark-provision">
                <div class="ark-provision-form">
                    <div class="ark-grid-2">
                        <div class="ark-field">
                            <label for="pv-tenant_id">Tenant</label>
                            <select id="pv-tenant_id"></select>
                        </div>
                        <div class="ark-field">
                            <label for="pv-client_name">Application name <span class="ark-req">required</span></label>
                            <input type="text" id="pv-client_name" placeholder="Billing Portal" />
                        </div>
                        <div class="ark-field">
                            <label for="pv-client_id">client_id</label>
                            <input type="text" id="pv-client_id" placeholder="derived from the name" />
                            <div class="ark-note">Refused if it is already taken — nothing is overwritten.</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="ark-field">
                            <label for="pv-application_type">application_type</label>
                            <select id="pv-application_type">
                                <option value="web">web</option>
                                <option value="spa">spa</option>
                                <option value="native">native</option>
                                <option value="service">service</option>
                            </select>
                        </div>
                        <div class="ark-field">
                            <label for="pv-user_name">User name or email <span class="ark-req">required</span></label>
                            <input type="text" id="pv-user_name" placeholder="jane@@example.com" />
                            <div class="ark-note">An existing account is reused and mapped to the new client.</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="ark-field">
                            <label for="pv-user_display_name">User's display name</label>
                            <input type="text" id="pv-user_display_name" placeholder="Jane Smith" />
                        </div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="ark-field">
                        <label for="pv-redirect_uris">redirect_uris</label>
                        <textarea id="pv-redirect_uris" spellcheck="false" placeholder="https://app.example.com/signin-oidc"></textarea>
                        <div class="ark-note">One per line. Can be added later in the client editor.</div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="ark-field">
                        <label for="pv-claims">Claims for the mapping</label>
                        <input type="text" id="pv-claims" placeholder="sub, name, email, email_verified" />
                        <div class="ark-note">Comma separated. Left blank, the identity claims are used.</div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="ark-field">
                        <label for="pv-client_logo">Application logo</label>
                        <div class="ark-logo-edit">
                            <span class="ark-logo-preview" id="pv-logo-preview" data-empty="true"></span>
                            <div class="ark-logo-edit-main">
                                <input type="text" id="pv-client_logo" placeholder="https://… or upload one" />
                                <div class="ark-note">
                                    <button type="button" class="ark-btn-ghost ark-btn-sm" id="pv-logo-pick">Upload image…</button>
                                    <button type="button" class="ark-btn-ghost ark-btn-sm" id="pv-logo-clear">Remove</button>
                                    <input type="file" id="pv-logo-file" accept="image/*" hidden />
                                    Appears on the sign-in page for this application.
                                </div>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="ark-check">
                        <input type="checkbox" id="pv-send_activation_email" />
                        <label for="pv-send_activation_email">Email an activation link instead of using the default password</label>
                    </div>
                    <div class="ark-provision-actions">
                        <button type="button" id="pv-submit">Provision</button>
                        <span class="ark-note" style="margin:0" id="pv-password-mode-note">
                            A new account signs in with <code>ark_oauth_server:DefaultPw</code> unless the box above is ticked.
                        </span>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <aside class="ark-provision-result" id="pv-result">
                    <h4>Result</h4>
                    <p class="ark-empty">Nothing provisioned yet.</p>
                </aside>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

    @*
        Activation. Both switches in one place, because "why can this person not sign in" is one
        question with two possible answers and chasing it across two grids is how the wrong one
        gets flipped. The form at the top is the API request itself — pick a target and a state —
        and the lists below it are the same operation applied to a row you can see the state of.
    *@
    <section class="ark-panel">
        <div class="ark-panel-head">
            <h2>Activation</h2>
            <span class="ark-hint">suspend an application or an account without deleting it — sign-ins are then refused with a message naming the level</span>
        </div>
        <div class="ark-panel-body">
            <div class="ark-activation-form">
                <div class="ark-grid-2">
                    <div class="ark-field">
                        <label for="av-target">Level</label>
                        <select id="av-target">
                            <option value="client">Application (client)</option>
                            <option value="user">Account (user)</option>
                        </select>
                    </div>
                    <div class="ark-field" id="av-tenant-field">
                        <label for="av-tenant_id">Tenant</label>
                        <select id="av-tenant_id"></select>
                    </div>
                    <div class="ark-field" id="av-client-field">
                        <label for="av-client_id">Application</label>
                        <select id="av-client_id"><option value="">Select an application</option></select>
                    </div>
                    <div class="ark-field" id="av-user-field" hidden>
                        <label for="av-user_name">Account</label>
                        <select id="av-user_name"><option value="">Select an account</option></select>
                    </div>
                    <div class="ark-field">
                        <label for="av-is_active">State</label>
                        <select id="av-is_active">
                            <option value="false">Deactivate</option>
                            <option value="true">Activate</option>
                        </select>
                    </div>
                    <div class="ark-field">
                        <label for="av-reason">Reason</label>
                        <input type="text" id="av-reason" placeholder="recorded in the audit trail" />
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div class="ark-provision-actions">
                    <button type="button" id="av-submit">Apply</button>
                    <span class="ark-note" style="margin:0">
                        Deactivating revokes what has already been handed out — a client loses its refresh
                        tokens, an account loses its sessions and refresh tokens as well.
                    </span>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="ark-activation-head">
                <h4>Current state</h4>
                <span class="ark-spacer"></span>
                <input type="search" id="act-filter" placeholder="Filter…" aria-label="Filter clients and users" />
            </div>
            <div class="ark-activation">
                <div class="ark-activation-col">
                    <h4>Applications <span class="ark-hint">client level</span></h4>
                    <div class="ark-activation-list" id="act-clients"></div>
                </div>
                <div class="ark-activation-col">
                    <h4>Accounts <span class="ark-hint">user level</span></h4>
                    <div class="ark-activation-list" id="act-users"></div>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

    @*
        The same two requests, as curl.

        It appears on its own as soon as a form has enough in it to be a valid request, and it
        carries the values that are in the forms right now — not a template with placeholders to
        fill in a second time. What it cannot read out of the page is the credential: the console's
        own session is an HttpOnly cookie, deliberately unreadable from script, so the command
        authenticates the way a script would instead — client_credentials against the machine
        client seeded beside the console. That secret exists only as a PBKDF2 hash, so the button
        below mints a new one and drops it straight into the command, which is the only moment
        anything can.
    *@
    <section class="ark-panel" id="cu-panel" hidden>
        <div class="ark-panel-head">
            <h2>The same thing, as curl</h2>
            <span class="ark-hint">built from the forms above, ready to paste into a shell or an onboarding script</span>
            <span class="ark-spacer"></span>
            <button type="button" class="ark-btn-ghost ark-btn-sm" id="cu-copy">Copy</button>
        </div>
        <div class="ark-panel-body">
            <div class="ark-grid-2">
                <div class="ark-field">
                    <label for="cu-client_id">Machine client</label>
                    <input type="text" id="cu-client_id" spellcheck="false" />
                    <div class="ark-note">A confidential client with the <code>client_credentials</code> grant.</div>
                </div>
                <div class="ark-field">
                    <label for="cu-client_secret">client_secret</label>
                    <input type="text" id="cu-client_secret" spellcheck="false" placeholder="not shown — secrets are stored hashed" />
                    <div class="ark-note">
                        <button type="button" class="ark-btn-ghost ark-btn-sm" id="cu-secret-reset">Generate a new secret</button>
                        Replaces the current one immediately, and is the only time it can be read.
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>
            <pre id="cu-out" class="ark-code"></pre>
            <div class="ark-note" id="cu-note"></div>
        </div>
    </section>
</main>

<div class="ark-toasts" id="ark-toasts" aria-live="polite"></div>

<script src="@ViewBag.ConsoleJs"></script>
</body>
</html>
